# The fewest obstacles removed to cross a grid.
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 2290, "Minimum Obstacle Removal to Reach Corner"): a
# grid of free cells and obstacles; walking from the top left corner to the
# bottom right one by steps up, down, left and right, how few obstacles must
# be removed? Asked as the set of the numbers at most K of the obstacles
# entered by the walks between the corners; K is the fewest, so the set is
# that one number.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style, the continuation the first
# parameter; a cell is the prelude's pair of two unary numerals, the row and
# the column; with the prelude's pair, first, second, natural_equal,
# natural_at_least, natural_difference, successor, and, bit_set_member
# (Hidden37Obstacles.obstaclesTerm in gezi/contest-corpora); top is no
# answer (corpora/library/top); + is the choice:
#
#     nth     = Y (\self. \xs. \n. xs nil (\h. \t. n h (\m. self t m)))
#     blocked = \r. \c. bit_set_member (nth Grid r) c
#     moves   = \r. \c. \f. r top (\m. f m c)
#                         + at_least LastRow (successor r) (f (successor r) c) top
#                         + c top (\m. f r m)
#                         + at_least LastColumn (successor c) (f r (successor c)) top
#     goal    = \p. and (equal (first p) LastRow) (equal (second p) LastColumn)
#     pred    = Y (\self. \n. n zero (\h. odd (self h)) (\h. h zero (\_. even h) (\_. even h)))
#     go      = Y (\self. \k. \e. \s. \p.
#                  goal p (k e) top
#                  + (\a. s top (\_. a) (\_. a))
#                      ((\s'. p (\r. \c. moves r c (\r'. \c'.
#                         blocked r' c' (e top (\d. self k d s' (pair r' c')))
#                                       (self k e s' (pair r' c'))))) (pred s)))
#     program = go (\e. difference K e) K S (pair 0 0)
#
# Grid is a Scott list of rows, each a Scott list of Booleans, true for an
# obstacle; the corners are free. S, the step budget, is the number of
# states, the cells times K + 1, written as a canonical binary numeral
# (zero, even, odd; pred is its predecessor), and each move spends one step.
# Without the budget a walk round the free cells reads ⊥ in DdLP96's
# reading (a choice is a meet and Y the least fixed point), the whole
# choice with it, and every closed βη normal form would be an answer.
# Directions: C (the grid's cycles; a reading that merges the states reads
# each once); F (a trap whose budget is one short: the walks find nothing,
# and the right answer is no normal form); D (the prelude's first, second, natural_equal, bit_set_member, the
# last behind a wrapper of the program); G (the budget); and the
# regression directions 3 (the nodes of a visited set search are pairs of
# numerals, not numerals) and 5 (the recursion takes its continuation first,
# and the steps are made by a closure, moves, that calls back its argument).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. The states are the pairs of a cell and the removals left.
# From (x, e): x the bottom right corner answers K - e; a step to a free
# neighbour y goes to (y, e), and a step to an obstacle y to (y, e - 1) when
# e > 0. The program's answers are the numerals of the
# answers of the states reachable from the top left corner with K left
# (Hidden37Obstacles.removalCounts, a search of that finite graph). Every
# answer is at least the fewest removals (Hidden37Obstacles.fewestRemovals),
# so for K the fewest the answers are {K}, and for K one less there is none.
#
# THE LEMMAS, on the definitions of the functions (problems/attractors and
# the prelude), not on their native references.
# ⌜n⌝ is the Scott unary numeral, ⌜0⌝ = λz.λs. z and ⌜n + 1⌝ = λz.λs. s ⌜n⌝,
# which unary/2 builds exactly, and successor n is one β step from
# λz.λs. s n; true = λt.λf. t and false = λt.λf. f; pair a b →β λf. f a b,
# first = λp. p (λl.λ_. l) and second = λp. p (λ_.λr. r), so first
# (pair a b) =β a and second (pair a b) =β b; a row is a Scott list of
# Booleans, the grid a Scott list of rows.
# - (Y) fixpoint G =β G (fixpoint G), two β steps.
# - (Equal) equal = Y (λs.λm.λn. m (n true (λ_. false))
#   (λm1. n false (λn1. s m1 n1))). By induction on m, equal ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β
#   true when m = n and false otherwise.
# - (AtLeast) at_least = Y (λs.λm.λn. n true (λn1. m false (λm1. s m1 n1))).
#   By induction on n, at_least ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β true when m >= n and false
#   otherwise.
# - (And) and a b = a b false: and true b =β b, and false b =β false.
# - (Nth) nth [x0, ..., xm] ⌜i⌝ =β xi for i =< m, by induction on i.
# - (Member) bit_set_member = Y (λs.λset.λi. set false (λb.λrest. i b
#   (λp. s rest p))). bit_set_member [b0, ..., bm] ⌜i⌝ =β bi for i =< m,
#   by induction on i. So blocked ⌜r⌝ ⌜c⌝ =β true exactly when the cell
#   (r, c) of the grid is an obstacle.
# - (Moves) moves ⌜r⌝ ⌜c⌝ f is β-equal to a choice of four members, in which
#   the member of each neighbour (r', c') of (r, c) inside the grid is
#   β-equal to f ⌜r'⌝ ⌜c'⌝, and every other member to top (a zero coordinate
#   selects top, and at_least selects top past the last row or column).
# - (Budget) e top (λd. M) with e =β ⌜j⌝ is β-equal to top when j = 0 and to
#   M[d := ⌜j - 1⌝] when j > 0.
# - (Steps) on a canonical binary numeral s > 0, pred s is the canonical
#   numeral of s - 1 (by induction on the digits: pred (2h) = 2 pred(h) + 1
#   for h ≥ 1, pred (2h + 1) = 2h, zero when h is), and s top (λ_. a) (λ_. a)
#   is top when s is zero and a otherwise.
# - (Difference) difference ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β ⌜m - n⌝ when m >= n (the induction
#   of the prelude's definition, as in the other problems of this group).
#
# So go k e s p, with p =β pair ⌜r⌝ ⌜c⌝, e =β ⌜j⌝ and s the binary numeral of
# t, is β-equal to a choice of two members: the first is β-equal to k e when
# (r, c) is the bottom right corner (Equal, And) and to top otherwise; the
# second is top when t = 0 and otherwise the choice of Moves, whose member for
# a neighbour (r', c') is β-equal to go k e s' (pair ⌜r'⌝ ⌜c'⌝), s' the
# numeral of t - 1, when it is free, and when it is an obstacle to top if
# j = 0 and to go k ⌜j - 1⌝ s' (pair ⌜r'⌝ ⌜c'⌝) if j > 0 (Member, Budget,
# Steps).
#
# PROOF, under Definition 14 (doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md): top ≃_O 0, so the
# program has the answers of the one with 0 in top's place, whose reductions terminate (the step budget
# falls at every move). Its answers are those of its selections, the terms got
# by choosing one member at every choice (the selection theorem), and a choice holding 0
# offers no member.
# - (⊇) Take a path in the graph of the states from (corner, K) to (bottom
#   right, e); a shortest one has fewer steps than there are states, so it
#   fits in the budget S. Choose, at each state, the move of the next step,
#   and at the end the first member: the selection reduces to
#   k ⌜e⌝ = difference ⌜K⌝ ⌜e⌝ =β ⌜K - e⌝ (Difference).
# - (⊆) A selection follows moves to neighbours inside the grid, an obstacle
#   paid by a removal, while steps are left, and either chooses the first
#   member at the bottom right corner, reducing to ⌜K - e⌝ for a reachable
#   state (bottom right, e), or meets 0 (the first member elsewhere, a move
#   off the grid, an obstacle with no removal left, the step budget spent)
#   and is no selection. So every answer is the answer of a
#   reachable state.
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: obstacles_point/6 below. The traps take the
# budget one short of the fewest, so they have no answer: a reading that
# answers some number there is wrong, and one that does not merge the states
# follows every walk within the step budget, exponentially many.
# Hidden37ObstaclesReadingTest checks the tabled weak head against
# Hidden37Obstacles.removalCounts on every grid of two rows and two or three
# columns at every budget from 0 to 2.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: the search over the pairs of a cell and the removals,
# with a visited set, or Dijkstra's algorithm on the grid with the cost of
# entering an obstacle 1 and of a free cell 0 (E. W. Dijkstra, A Note on Two
# Problems in Connexion with Graphs, Numer. Math. 1959,
# doi:10.1007/BF01386390); tabled evaluation keyed by the value of the cell
# and the budget (W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996,
# doi:10.1145/227595.227597).

#   THE SUBTASKS: obstacles_point(?Point, ?Rows, ?Columns, ?Seed, ?Slack,
#   ?Kind): a Rows by Columns grid, each cell an obstacle with probability
#   three in five from a linear congruential sequence of Seed, the corners
#   free; the budget the fewest removals plus Slack.
#   Read on the budgeted program (a probe of 2026-11-12, a
#   scratch test not committed, contest-framework.slice, CPUs 0 and 1;
#   the steps of `NormalForms.normalForms` within 20,000,000):
#   tabling(weak_head) reads four by five (the fewest 3) in 20,356 steps
#   and runs out of tables at about 110,000 steps on twenty, thirty two,
#   forty eight and sixty four; weak_head runs out of steps on all five;
#   the trap of four reads no answer in 58,517 (weak_head) and 2,977
#   (tabling), and the trap of twenty is read by neither. The step budget
#   is part of the state a reading's table keys on, so a table keyed by the
#   spelling merges two states only at the same steps left, which is why
#   the tabled reading stops at twenty where it read forty eight on the
#   unbudgeted program (2026-09-30). The sizes stay (2026-11-12):
#   the references are the native removalCounts, not readings, and the
#   large grids ask a reading to merge the states by value, which is the
#   family's question. The native reference answers every point in
#   milliseconds.
